<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!DOCTYPE plist PUBLIC "-//Apple//DTD PLIST 1.0//EN" "http://www.apple.com/DTDs/PropertyList-1.0.dtd">
<plist version="1.0">
<dict>
	<!-- Sandbox stays off (§10): Accessibility and the mediaremote-adapter's Perl helper are both
	     incompatible with it, which is also why the Mac App Store is not a channel for this app. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.app-sandbox</key>
	<false/>
	<!-- WeatherKit, for the glance. The first developer entitlement Isleta has ever claimed, and the
	     reason Config/Isleta.provisionprofile now exists: an entitlement that no embedded
	     provisioning profile authorises is not a warning and not a signing error — `codesign`
	     accepts it silently and the app exits 137 on launch, with AMFI writing "Unsatisfied
	     Entitlements" to the kernel log and nothing at all to ours. No stdout, no stderr, no .ips
	     crash report. It is the 1.3.0 Bluetooth abort's shape, and like that one it is invisible to
	     every check that runs from a shell.

	     Two consequences that outlive this line. Isleta now has a provisioning profile, and
	     Gatekeeper validates it at *every launch* — so its expiry is not a feature degrading, it is
	     the app refusing to open. And editing the App ID's capabilities on developer.apple.com
	     invalidates every existing profile for it, so the profile must be regenerated and
	     re-embedded whenever that page is touched. Tools/release.sh checks both directions before it
	     builds anything. -->
	<key>com.apple.developer.weatherkit</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- Hardened Runtime's **Resource Access** entitlements, and their absence is a silent failure.
	     `ENABLE_HARDENED_RUNTIME = YES` (see the project) means the system refuses a protected
	     resource unless the app declares it here. Refuses *silently*: measured 2026-08-23 against
	     the shipped 2.0.0 build, `requestFullAccessToEvents` answered **granted=false with a nil
	     error in 50 ms**, no dialog appeared, the status stayed `notDetermined`, and **tccd's own
	     log never mentioned Isleta at all** — the request is not refused by the daemon, it never
	     reaches it. `CLLocationManager.requestWhenInUseAuthorization()` failed identically, with
	     `locationd` equally silent.

	     That is indistinguishable from a missing usage string, which is what sent this hunting
	     through TCC records, LaunchServices caches and bundle identities for half an hour. The
	     usage strings were correct in `Config/Isleta-Info.plist` the whole time; a usage string
	     says *why* the app wants a resource, and these say the app is *allowed to ask*. Both are
	     required, and only one of them fails loudly.

	     The control that settled it: an ad-hoc probe with the same usage string and **no hardened
	     runtime** prompted and was granted in 2.4 s, from three different bundle identifiers.

	     These are `com.apple.security.*`, so they are consumed by the sandbox and the hardened
	     runtime rather than by AMFI — unlike `com.apple.developer.weatherkit` below, they need no
	     provisioning-profile authorisation and cannot cause the exit-137 abort. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.personal-information.calendars</key>
	<true/>
	<key>com.apple.security.personal-information.location</key>
	<true/>
	<!-- Bluetooth, for the device-connected island — and it was missing while the two above were
	     present, which is how the same silent failure shipped twice.

	     It does not fail the way calendars and location did. Those answered "not granted" in 50 ms;
	     this **never answers at all**. Measured 2026-08-29 on macOS 27.0, by `sample` against a
	     Developer ID signed build launched with `open -a`:
	     `+[IOBluetoothHostController defaultController]` runs a `dispatch_once` that builds
	     `IOBluetoothCoreBluetoothCoordinator`, whose initialiser waits on a dispatch semaphore for
	     `bluetoothd` — and with the hardened runtime on and this key absent, that reply never
	     comes. The wait has no timeout. Called from the main thread it deadlocked the whole app
	     inside `applicationDidFinishLaunching`: no island, no crash report, no log line, and every
	     source after Bluetooth in `SourceHub.entries` never started.

	     tccd never mentioned `kTCCServiceBluetoothAlways` for Isleta at all, exactly as the block
	     above describes — the request never reaches the daemon. It was invisible from a shell for
	     the reason CLAUDE.md gives about `open -a`: the same probe run from Terminal is attributed
	     to Terminal, whose grant is already decided, and answered in 3 ms.

	     `IOBluetoothDeviceMonitor` now wakes the coordinator off the main thread regardless, so the
	     app can no longer be hung by this. That is a seatbelt, not the fix; this key is the fix. -->
	<key>com.apple.security.device.bluetooth</key>
	<true/>
</dict>
</plist>
